<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#cipollino-marble">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cipollino marble</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as marmor Carystium</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cipollino is a green-and-white banded marble quarried in antiquity near Karystos in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. Its onion-like layers gave it its Italian name. It was widely used by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> for columns and wall veneers in buildings such as the Temple of Antoninus and Faustina in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cipollino-marble.jpg" alt="Cipollino marble" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MM · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MarmoCipollino_FustoBasMassenzioRoma.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
